    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Hypertension, stage 2 (I10)</content>. Controlled in the clinic and at home. [S]<list><item>Continue amlodipine 10 mg and valsartan 160 mg.</item><item>Potassium and creatinine in about two months (six months on valsartan).</item><item>Lipids, glucose and urine albumin at the next preventive visit; echocardiogram in about eight months to look for regression of the hypertrophy.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Alcohol use disorder, moderate (F10.20)</content>. Drinking within his own goal and in the low-risk range (AUDIT 7), with GGT normal; too soon to call remission, which needs at least three months without the criteria. [S]<list><item>Continue naltrexone for at least a few more months, then decide together whether to stop.</item><item>Plan for the period after a fight, when he says the pull to drink is strongest.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Office blood pressure (seated, large cuff, average of two readings), weight and BMI, heart sounds</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>122/76 mmHg; weight 97.0 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Out-of-office blood pressure (ambulatory or home monitoring)</td><td>At diagnosis and after each treatment change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Home average 120/76 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Potassium, creatinine and eGFR</td><td>Baseline; two to four weeks after starting an ARB; then every six months on an ARB</td><td>Due</td><td>Next in about two months, six months after starting valsartan</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lipids, glucose or A1c, urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio, TSH</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly (TSH at diagnosis)</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Done at intake; next at the preventive visit next year</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Echocardiogram</td><td>At diagnosis for stage 2 hypertension or suspected LVH; repeat as indicated</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Repeat in about eight months</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antihypertensive tolerance (ankle edema, dizziness) and adherence by teach-back</td><td>Each follow-up and each dose change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No edema today; no dizziness; teach-back correct</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Fitness to compete (AHA/ACC athlete statement)</td><td>At diagnosis and before returning to competition</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Cleared by the commission physician; blood pressure checked before each bout</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Drinking (AUDIT-C, drinks per week, drinking days)</td><td>Every follow-up during treatment; full AUDIT at about four months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Full AUDIT 7, from 16; about 8 drinks a week</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Liver tests (AST, ALT, GGT)</td><td>Baseline; about two months after starting naltrexone or cutting down; then as indicated</td><td>Completed today</td><td>GGT 41 U/L, now normal</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Naltrexone: nausea, liver tests, and opioid-blockade wallet card</td><td>Each follow-up after starting</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Well tolerated; wallet card carried</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>Yearly with hypertension; sooner with symptoms</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 2, item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review (structured questions) and safety plan</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide; safety plan kept</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Health literacy: teach-back, picture card, read-aloud consent, voice reminders</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Teach-back correct; the adult reading class offered again, and he will decide after his next fight</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Confirm or rule out hypertension with out-of-office readings before any medicine. Outcome by 1969-06-23: Ambulatory monitoring confirmed stage 2 hypertension (daytime 146/94 mmHg) [S]</item><item>Blood pressure under 130/80 mmHg in the clinic and at home by four months. Outcome by 1969-10-20: Clinic 122/76 mmHg; home average 120/76 mmHg on amlodipine 10 mg and valsartan 160 mg [S]</item><item>Drink within the goal he set: no more than 14 drinks a week and no more than 4 on any day, and none in the two weeks before a fight. Outcome by 1969-10-20: About 8 drinks a week with no day above 3; AUDIT 7; GGT normal [S]</item><item>Explain his medicines and blood pressure plan back in his own words. Outcome by 1969-07-21: Teach-back correct at four weeks with the picture card [S]</item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Influenza vaccine, 1969-1970 season [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2025 AHA/ACC/AANP/AAPA/ABC/ACCP/ACPM/AGS/AMA/ASPC/NMA/PCNA/SGIM Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults (2025); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, hypertension and depression monographs (2026); Eligibility and Disqualification Recommendations for Competitive Athletes With Cardiovascular Abnormalities: Task Force 6: Hypertension (Black HR, et al.) (2015); The Healthcare Professional&apos;s Core Resource on Alcohol (2026); The American Psychiatric Association Practice Guideline for the Pharmacological Treatment of Patients With Alcohol Use Disorder (2018); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); AHRQ Health Literacy Universal Precautions Toolkit, 3rd Edition (2024); Adult Immunization Schedule by Age (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Potassium and creatinine in about two months. Review in three months, sooner after any bout with a head injury. Preventive visit in ten months. Call 988 or the clinic at any time if thoughts of suicide arise. [S]</paragraph></text>

